  "number": "6.16",
  "title": "Deep-unit Evens norm.",
  "body": " Deep-unit Evens norm   Let be an unramified quadratic extension of finite dyadic local fields and put . Then    Lean   We translate the Evens norm into the degree-two Evens Kahn identity , reduce the trace forms to Hilbert symbols , and then cancel the remaining terms by norm and Steinberg relations .  By , the Evens norm is represented by the explicit two-point graph cocycle  . The index-two Evens Kahn formula identifies this normalization with the degree-two term in the relative Stiefel Whitney identity  ,    Take a class in and choose from the outset a representative Write , where is the unramified quadratic square class, and write If then In particular the divisions by below are legitimate. The trace forms diagonalize as Taking degree two in first gives We now keep every Hilbert-symbol cancellation visible. Bilinearity and give Consequently Thus    In more detail: the Kummer coordinates are part of the proof, not additional arithmetic hypotheses. Since the residue characteristic is not the field characteristic, an unramified quadratic extension has a presentation with , and every deep unit can be written uniquely as . The index-two condition ensures ; together with , this shows that is nonzero and that the divisions in the displayed diagonalization are legitimate.  Because is unramified and , one has . From , Here is a unit and . Hensel's lemma , applied to at , therefore gives . Thus and have the same square class.   Unramifiedness is used here in two distinct ways. Equality of value groups gives the depth estimate that makes a square by Hensel's lemma. In the remaining line, surjectivity of the norm on units kills the symbols involving ; the Steinberg relation then reduces the answer to two copies of , which cancel in characteristic two.  Every unit of is a norm from its unramified quadratic extension, and . Hence Formula becomes The Steinberg relation gives . Finally : if , then is a square; otherwise . Consequently , and the two terms cancel.   "
