  "type": "Remark",
  "number": "C.18",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  The congruence clause is necessary and not merely convenient. Its two cokernel coordinates measure exactly the top orientation deviation digits of in the two untwisted slots, with the slots transposed, so the defect is reachable precisely when the clause holds. The sharpest illustration is the group , which has the same rank, the same and the same abelianization as , and whose orientation differs only in the image. For it the analogue of is empty, because the relevant orientation value is rather than modulo , so no triple can achieve the required congruence depth. This is the point at which the surjectivity hypothesis of is consumed.   "
